wrk:2722043
    a rdac:C10001 ;
    rdaxd:P00018 "http://entedit.org/work/2722043",
        "https://www.isfdb.org/cgi-bin/title.cgi?2722043" ;
    rdawd:P10223 "La journée d'un journaliste américain en 2890"@fr ;
    rdfs:label "La journée d'un journaliste américain en 2890 (Verne, Jules; Verne, Michel)" ;
    rdawd:P10219 "1890" ;
    rdawd:P10330 "The following publication history is based on Arthur B. Evans's published research.\nThe original version of this story was published as \"In the Year 2889\" in the English-language magazine ''The Forum'' (New York, USA) in February 1889. Only Jules Verne was listed as the author. However, based on subsequently published correspondence between Jules Verne and his publisher, the text had been actually written by Michel Verne, Jules Verne's son. It's currently unknown whether Michel Verne wrote this version in French and had it translated into English.\nThe French text of this story was then significantly revised by Jules Verne as \"La Journée d'un journaliste américain en 2890\". It was published, again as by Jules Verne alone, in three different French magazines in 1890-1891.\nThe third version of the story was re-written by Michel Verne as part of his project to update and publish Jules Verne's uncollected and unpublished works after Jules's death. It appeared as \"Au XXIXe siècle : La Journée d'un journaliste américain en 2889\" in the French-language collection \"Hier et Demain\" (1910), again as by Jules Verne alone. It reverted some changes made in the second (1890) version back the original 1889 version (e.g. the year was changed from 2890 back to 2889), retained some changes made in 1890 (e.g. the name of the planet Gandini vs. Olympus) and introduced new changes (e.g. life expectancy changed from 52 in the 1889 version to 58 in the 1890 version and to 68 in the 1910 version).\nFor information on identifying Jules Verne translations, please see this wiki page." ;
    entedit:P01 vtp:V1000 ;
    entedit:P02 entedit:T02 ;
    rdawo:P10061 per:159,
        per:120544 .

